guide("/septic-for-golf-courses-marinas-campgrounds/","Septic Pumping for Golf Courses, Marinas, and Campgrounds | Express Septic",
 "Commercial septic pumping for golf course clubhouses, marinas, and campgrounds in Southwest Florida. Quoted for your tank and use. Call (239) 451-3261.",
 "Septic pumping for golf courses, marinas, and campgrounds",
 "<p><strong>Golf course clubhouses, marina restrooms, and campgrounds send a burst of wastewater through a septic tank on weekends, tournament days, and seasonal holidays, then sit quieter midweek.</strong> This page is for grounds superintendents, marina managers, and campground operators who need those tanks on a schedule that matches the calendar, not a household 3 to 5 year rule of thumb. Churches, schools, and RV parks have a separate page at <a href='/commercial-septic-churches-schools-rv-parks/'>commercial septic for churches, schools, and RV parks</a>. Call <a href='tel:"+TEL+"'>"+PHONE+"</a> with the property type and how you use it.</p>"
 "<h2>Why do these facilities need a seasonal plan?</h2>"
 "<p>A clubhouse kitchen and locker rooms can be quiet on a Tuesday and packed after a Saturday shotgun start. A marina restroom fills when boats are in and the restaurant is serving. A campground can be nearly empty in September and full in February. The tank does not get a larger capacity because the parking lot is full. A recurring pump-out timed before known peaks is more useful than waiting for an odor at the 18th green or the dock. Commercial pumping is quoted for the tank and its use. The published residential starting price of $399.99 applies to a standard 1–3 bedroom home, not automatically to these systems.</p>"
 "<h2>What about kitchens, dump stations, and boat facilities?</h2>"
 "<p>A clubhouse or marina kitchen may have a grease trap or interceptor that should be pumped on its own schedule. A 1,000-gallon unit is $695; other sizes are quoted. That work is described on <a href='/grease-trap-pumping/'>grease trap and interceptor pumping</a>. If a campground has an RV dump station tied to a tank, tell us; that kind of load changes how often the tank needs to be emptied, and we will talk about it when you call rather than inventing a frequency here. We pump septic tanks; we do not treat this page as a bilge or marina-waste service beyond the building's sanitary system.</p>"
 "<h2>How should a manager plan access on a large property?</h2>"
 "<p>Courses and camps often have long internal roads, soft shoulders after rain, and lids hidden in landscaping around a clubhouse. Tell us about cart-path only areas, locked service gates, and whether a second entrance is better for the truck. Locating and digging a buried lid is $100, plus $50 for each additional hour. A riser at $299.99 each is often worth it on a site that will be pumped on a route. For very large parcels, the access notes on <a href='/septic-for-acreage-ranches-farms/'>acreage, ranches, and farms</a> may also help, even though that page is written for household tanks on rural lots.</p>"
 "<h2>What should staff do during a weekend backup?</h2>"
 "<p>Close the affected restrooms if you can, stop adding water, and call <a href='tel:"+TEL+"'>"+PHONE+"</a>. After-hours pumping is $599.99. Do not use the online booking form during an overflow. We pump both compartments when we pump and clean an effluent filter at no charge if the tank has one. If a tournament is the next morning, say so when you call. Drain field work, if the field is the problem, is quoted after we inspect the system. Waterfront or canal-side clubhouses should also watch for the patterns on <a href='/septic-for-waterfront-and-canal-homes/'>waterfront and canal homes</a>.</p>"
 "<h2>Can we book routine service ahead of a season?</h2>"
 "<p>Yes. Request a planned visit through <a href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>ServiceM8</a> or call, and tell us the first busy weekend you care about. We confirm the time before it is set. We serve these properties in Lee, Collier, and Charlotte County, plus LaBelle, including seasonal sites that sit quieter in the wetter months and fill in winter.</p>"
 "<p><a class='btn primary' href='tel:"+TEL+"'>Call "+PHONE+"</a> <a class='btn ghost' href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>Book routine service on ServiceM8<span class='sr'> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></p>",
 GM_FAQ, "Golf courses, marinas, and campgrounds")

EV_FAQ=[
 ("Do you pump septic tanks at wedding barns and event venues?","Yes, when we can reach the lids. Event-property tanks are quoted for the system and its use. Call (239) 451-3261 with the event calendar and access notes."),
 ("If we also need portable restrooms, is that Express?","No. Express pumps the venue's septic tank. Portable restrooms are handled by our sister company, <a href='https://www.luxrestroompro.com' rel='noopener'>Luxury Portable Restroom Rentals</a>. The two services are scheduled separately."),
 ("Should we pump before every wedding?","Not automatically. If a pump-out is due, or a busy stretch of events is coming and the last service was a long time ago, pump before that stretch. We do not invent a rule that every single event requires its own trip."),
 ("A backup started during a reception. What then?","Stop water use where you can, keep guests away from spilled sewage, and call (239) 451-3261. After-hours pumping is $599.99. Do not use the online booking form during an overflow."),
]
guide("/septic-for-event-venues-and-wedding-barns/","Septic Pumping for Event Venues and Wedding Barns in SWFL | Express Septic",
 "Septic pumping for event venues and wedding barns in Southwest Florida, quoted for your tank and use. For portable restrooms, see sister company Luxury Portable Restroom Rentals. Call (239) 451-3261.",
 "Septic pumping for event venues and wedding barns",
 "<p><strong>A wedding barn or event venue can be nearly empty on a weekday and then send a full reception's worth of water through the septic tank on a Saturday night.</strong> This page is about pumping the venue's own septic system and planning around the event calendar. It is not a portable-restroom catalog. If an event also needs portable restrooms, our sister company, <a href='https://www.luxrestroompro.com' rel='noopener'>Luxury Portable Restroom Rentals</a>, handles that work. Express pumps the tank; the two companies are scheduled separately. Call <a href='tel:"+TEL+"'>"+PHONE+"</a> about the septic side.</p>"
 "<h2>Why do venues need a plan that follows the booking calendar?</h2>"
 "<p>The tank does not know that 200 people are about to use the restrooms during cocktail hour. If the last pump-out was a long time ago, a clustered season of weddings or holiday parties can be the weekend drains gurgle. The EPA's household 3 to 5 year range is a poor sole guide for a building that is used in spikes. A better approach is to pump before a busy stretch when the tank is due, then stay on a schedule that matches how often the venue actually fills up. Commercial or venue tanks are quoted for the system and its use. The $399.99 residential starting price is for a standard 1–3 bedroom home and does not automatically apply here.</p>"
 "<h2>When should portable restrooms be part of the plan?</h2>"
 "<p>If indoor restrooms and the septic tank cannot reasonably handle the headcount, portable units can take pressure off the system. That is not an Express service on this page. Our sister company, <a href='https://www.luxrestroompro.com' rel='noopener'>Luxury Portable Restroom Rentals</a>, is the place to ask about that equipment. Using portables does not cancel the need to pump a tank that is already due, and pumping the tank does not replace restrooms the building does not have. Tell both sides about the event date so the yard is not trying to host a truck and a delivery in the same narrow drive at the same hour.</p>"
 "<h2>What about catering kitchens and grease?</h2>"
 "<p>A venue kitchen, or a caterer who ties into your sinks, can send grease into a trap or, if there is no trap, toward a septic tank that is not meant to be a grease interceptor. If you have a grease trap or interceptor, a 1,000-gallon unit is $695 to pump and other sizes are quoted; see <a href='/grease-trap-pumping/'>grease trap and interceptor pumping</a>. Ask caterers not to pour grease down the drains. A backup during a reception is far more expensive in goodwill than a planned visit midweek.</p>"
 "<h2>How should we plan access on event day versus a prep day?</h2>"
 "<p>Pump midweek when the barn is empty, not at ceremony time. Tell us about lawn parking, tents, dance floors, and whether lids sit under a rented floor or a flower install. Locating and digging a buried lid is $100, plus $50 for each additional hour. A riser at $299.99 each makes later visits less likely to collide with a setup crew. Routine work can be requested through <a href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>ServiceM8</a> with the event date in the notes; we confirm the time. Builders or tent companies working the site should keep equipment off the drain field; see also <a href='/septic-service-for-builders-and-contractors/'>builders and contractors</a>.</p>"
 "<h2>What if something backs up during the event?</h2>"
 "<p>Stop water use where you can, keep guests away from spilled sewage, and call <a href='tel:"+TEL+"'>"+PHONE+"</a>. After-hours pumping is $599.99. Do not use the online form during an overflow. We will tell you what we saw once the tank is open. Drain field work, if needed, is quoted after we inspect the system. For ordinary holiday hosting in a home, not a venue, use <a href='/holiday-and-guest-septic/'>holiday and guest season septic care</a> instead of this page.</p>"
 "<p><a class='btn primary' href='tel:"+TEL+"'>Call "+PHONE+"</a> <a class='btn ghost' href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>Book routine service on ServiceM8<span class='sr'> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></p>",
 EV_FAQ, "Event venues and wedding barns")

CS_FAQ=[
 ("Do you pump septic tanks on active construction sites?","Yes, when we can reach the lids safely. Tell us about mud, fences, overhead work, and who will meet the truck. Call (239) 451-3261."),
 ("Is a construction-site pump-out priced like a house visit?","A planned residential pump-out starts at $399.99 for a standard 1–3 bedroom home. Work tied to a construction site, a temporary facility, or a tank we have not seen is discussed when we know access and tank size."),
 ("Can the crew use the existing house tank as a dump for the whole job?","Only if that is really the plan and the tank can handle it. A remodel crew can fill a tank faster than the family who lived there. Talk to us rather than assuming the tank will last until the certificate of occupancy."),
 ("Who should be on site when you arrive?","Someone who can unlock gates, point out lids or known utilities, and keep equipment off the hose path and the drain field."),
]
guide("/septic-for-construction-sites/","Septic Pumping for Construction Sites in Southwest Florida | Express Septic",
 "Septic tank pumping on Southwest Florida construction and remodel sites: access, temporary use of an existing tank, and keeping equipment off the drain field. Call (239) 451-3261.",
 "Septic pumping for construction sites",
 "<p><strong>A construction or remodel site can need the septic tank pumped because crews are using the existing house system, because lids must be opened before the yard is closed up, or because someone finally found the tank under a pile of fill.</strong> This page is about those job-site visits. The broader guidance for protecting a field and coordinating with a builder is on <a href='/septic-service-for-builders-and-contractors/'>septic service for builders and contractors</a>. This page stays on the site itself: access, temporary load, and when to call. Call <a href='tel:"+TEL+"'>"+PHONE+"</a> with the address, the superintendent's number, and what the tank is being used for.</p>"
 "<h2>When does a job site need a pump-out?</h2>"
 "<p>Call when a house stays occupied through a remodel, when temporary facilities are tied into the existing tank, when the tank must be opened before a driveway, pool, or landscape package buries the lids, or when drains have already slowed with more people on site than the house usually has. A planned residential pump-out starts at $399.99 for a standard 1–3 bedroom home. Site-specific access or a tank we have not seen is discussed when we know the layout. Do not wait until the day of a pour or an inspection if you already know the tank is due.</p>"
 "<h2>How should we handle access in mud, fencing, and active trades?</h2>"
 "<p>We need a path for the truck and the hose, a place that is not blocked by a delivery, and a person who knows where the lids are, or who can admit that nobody knows. Locating and digging a buried lid is $100, plus $50 for each additional hour. Tell us about temporary fencing, drop-offs, overhead lifts, and soft sand after rain. If the drain field is still in place, it is not a staging pad, a soil-stockpile, or a parking lot for pickups. Compaction and broken laterals are much harder to undo than a marked-off corner of the lot.</p>"
 "<h2>What is risky about using the existing tank for the whole crew?</h2>"
 "<p>A family of three and a crew of a dozen plus a trash-filled remodel kitchen are not the same load. Wipes, drywall mud washout, paint, and solvents do not belong in the tank. If the project will last months, put pumping on the construction calendar rather than discovering a backup the week the owners move back in. We pump both compartments and clean an effluent filter at no charge if the tank has one. After-hours pumping is $599.99 if the site already has an overflow; do not use the online booking form during a backup.</p>"
 "<h2>How do we coordinate with the superintendent or the plumber?</h2>"
 "<p>Have one person call with gate codes, the preferred window, and any inspection deadline. Routine visits can be requested through <a href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>ServiceM8</a>; we confirm the time. Plumbers who need the tank opened so they can do their own work can also use <a href='/septic-pumping-for-plumbers/'>septic pumping for plumbers</a>. If a drain field is already failing, a pump-out will not finish the civil scope for you; <a href='/drain-field-replacement/'>drain field work is quoted after inspection</a>.</p>"
 "<h2>What should happen before the yard is finished?</h2>"
 "<p>If lids will disappear under new sod, a patio, or a driveway, consider a riser at $299.99 each while the tank is still easy to reach. Mark the field so the landscape crew does not plant trees on it. For planting and hardscape rules aimed at those trades, see <a href='/septic-for-landscapers-and-pool-builders/'>landscapers and pool builders</a>. We serve job sites in Lee, Collier, and Charlotte County, plus LaBelle.</p>"
 "<p><a class='btn primary' href='tel:"+TEL+"'>Call "+PHONE+"</a> <a class='btn ghost' href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>Book routine service on ServiceM8<span class='sr'> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></p>",
 CS_FAQ, "Construction sites")

