guide("/septic-for-insurance-and-restoration/","Septic Pumping for Insurance and Restoration After a Sewage Backup | Express Septic",
 "Coordination for insurance adjusters and restoration companies after a sewage backup in a Southwest Florida home on a septic tank. After-hours pumping is $599.99. Call (239) 451-3261.",
 "Septic pumping for insurance and restoration companies",
 "<p><strong>When a house on a septic tank has a sewage backup, the restoration crew and the adjuster often need the tank pumped before the indoor work can move, or they need to know whether the tank was simply full.</strong> This page is for those coordination calls. We pump the tank and tell you what we saw. We are not a restoration contractor, and this page does not claim that we file insurance claims or automatically bill a carrier. Call <a href='tel:"+TEL+"'>"+PHONE+"</a> with the address, a site contact, and whether sewage is still coming up.</p>"
 "<h2>What should happen first on a backup that may become a claim?</h2>"
 "<p>The occupants should stop running water and stay away from spilled sewage. Someone should call us if the tank has not been opened yet. After-hours pumping is $599.99. Do not use the online booking form during an active overflow. The homeowner-facing steps are also on <a href='/emergency-septic-backup/'>emergency septic backup</a>. If the overflow started with a storm, <a href='/septic-backup-after-heavy-rain/'>backup after heavy rain</a> describes that pattern. Pumping the tank is often the first mechanical step; it is not the indoor drying scope.</p>"
 "<h2>What will Express do, and what will we not do?</h2>"
 "<p>We locate the lid if needed, pump both compartments, and clean the effluent filter at no charge if the tank has one. Locating and digging a buried lid is $100, plus $50 for each additional hour. We tell the site contact what we observed. We do not pull wet drywall, run dehumidifiers, pack out contents, or write a cause-and-origin report in the insurance sense. If the drain field appears to be part of the problem, further work is quoted after we inspect the system, not estimated from a claim note. A planned residential pump-out, when the visit is not an after-hours emergency overflow, starts at $399.99 for a standard 1–3 bedroom home.</p>"
 "<h2>How should payment and authorization be handled?</h2>"
 "<p>Be prepared to tell us who the customer is before we roll. Do not assume we will bill the insurance company directly. The owner, the restoration company, or another authorized party should arrange payment according to the published prices that apply to the visit. If you need a simple description of those prices for a file, the <a href='/southwest-florida-septic-cost-guide/'>Southwest Florida septic service cost guide</a> lists what we publish and what we only quote after inspection. We can discuss the visit with an adjuster on site; we still need a clear authorization.</p>"
 "<h2>What information helps us and helps your file?</h2>"
 "<p>The last known pump-out date, whether an alarm sounded, whether the backup followed heavy rain, and whether one fixture or the whole house was involved. Photos of the yard are useful for your own file, but we will not quote a new drain field from a photograph. If the house is in a claim because of a storm or a flood, wait until lids are reachable and the lot is not under standing water; see <a href='/hurricane-and-flood-septic-prep/'>hurricane and flood septic prep</a>.</p>"
 "<h2>How do we schedule once the overflow has stopped?</h2>"
 "<p>If the backup is under control and you need a planned follow-up pump-out or an inspection with pump-out ($549.99), call or <a href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>request a routine time on ServiceM8</a>. We confirm the appointment. We serve these coordination calls in Lee, Collier, and Charlotte County, plus LaBelle, 24 hours a day.</p>"
 "<p><a class='btn primary' href='tel:"+TEL+"'>Call "+PHONE+"</a> <a class='btn ghost' href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>Book routine service on ServiceM8<span class='sr'> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></p>",
 IR_FAQ, "Insurance and restoration")

LP_FAQ=[
 ("Can we plant trees over a drain field to help it 'drink' the water?","No. Trees and large shrubs can send roots into the field and compact or shade it in unhelpful ways. Grass is the better cover. Call (239) 451-3261 if you need the field located before a planting plan."),
 ("Can a pool deck, a shed, or a patio sit on the drain field?","Do not build or set heavy structures on the field. Parking, pads, and decks can compact soil and damage pipes. Locate the tank and field before you bid the work."),
 ("Who should call if we uncover a lid or a wet area mid-job?","Stop work in that footprint and have the owner call (239) 451-3261, or call with the owner's permission. Do not trench through a suspected field to 'drain it.'"),
 ("Do you install landscaping or build pools?","No. We pump septic tanks and can help you keep the system clear of the new work. Pool owners can also read <a href='/pools-and-septic-systems/'>pools and septic systems</a>."),
]
guide("/septic-for-landscapers-and-pool-builders/","Drain Field Rules for Landscapers and Pool Builders in SWFL | Express Septic",
 "What landscapers and pool builders should not plant, pave, or build over a septic drain field in Southwest Florida. Locate the field before you bid. Call (239) 451-3261.",
 "What landscapers and pool builders should not plant or build over a drain field",
 "<p><strong>A landscaping plan or a new pool can quietly ruin a septic drain field if the field is treated as leftover yard.</strong> This page is for landscapers, irrigation crews, and pool builders working in Southwest Florida. It is about what not to plant, pave, or set on the field, and how to find out where that field is before you bid. It is not a homeowner pool-draining guide; that is <a href='/pools-and-septic-systems/'>pools and septic systems</a>. Job-site pumping and equipment traffic are also covered for general contractors on <a href='/septic-service-for-builders-and-contractors/'>builders and contractors</a> and <a href='/septic-for-construction-sites/'>construction sites</a>. Call <a href='tel:"+TEL+"'>"+PHONE+"</a> if the owner needs the tank or lids located before your work starts.</p>"
 "<h2>What should never go on top of the drain field?</h2>"
 "<p>Do not park, drive, or stage equipment on it. Do not set a shed, a cabana, a fire pit, a berm, or a heavy decorative wall on it. Do not pour a deck, a spa pad, or a pool equipment slab on it. Do not pile fill or stockpile stone on it. The field needs unsaturated soil and intact laterals. Weight compacting that soil, and construction traffic during a pool or landscape project, can show up later as a soggy footprint or a backup in the house. If you do not know where the field is, find it before the first delivery, not after the pad is formed.</p>"
 "<h2>What should not be planted over the field?</h2>"
 "<p>Skip trees and large shrubs in the drain-field footprint. Roots look for moisture and can invade lines. Grass is the usual, appropriate cover. Do not plant a garden that needs constant tilling, and do not use the field as the place to hide a row of ficus or a hedge to screen a pool. Irrigation that soaks the field on a timer adds water the soil may not be able to take, especially in the rainy season. Divert roof downspouts and irrigation runoff away from the field rather than toward it. We are not giving a botanical species list as a scientific inventory; the practical rule is to keep woody roots and extra water off that footprint.</p>"
 "<h2>How do we locate the tank and field before we bid?</h2>"
 "<p>Ask the owner for as-builts, a prior pump-out sketch, or a survey. If lids are buried, the owner can have us locate and dig for $100, plus $50 for each additional hour; details are on <a href='/cant-find-septic-tank-lid/'>can't find the septic tank lid</a>. A riser at $299.99 each can be installed once the lid is open so later service does not start with a shovel through your new sod. Do not probe wildly with a mini excavator. If you uncover a lid or a wet, smelly area mid-job, stop in that footprint and get the owner to call us.</p>"
 "<h2>What about grade changes, retaining walls, and drainage work?</h2>"
 "<p>Raising or cutting grade over the field can bury lids, change how water moves, and smother a system that was working. A retaining wall or a swale that points stormwater at the field can saturate it. If your scope includes regrading around a pool cage or a new lanai, treat the field as a constraint in the plan, not as fill you can borrow. After a heavy rain, a stressed field is more obvious; that is a reason to leave it alone, not a reason to trench it. Homeowners reading wet spots can use <a href='/wet-spots-green-grass-over-drain-field/'>wet spots and green grass</a>.</p>"
 "<h2>When should the owner pump before your project starts?</h2>"
 "<p>If the tank is due, or records are missing, a pump-out before a long project is a good idea, especially if crews will be using the house. A planned residential pump-out starts at $399.99 (standard 1–3 bed). The owner can <a href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>request a routine visit on ServiceM8</a> or call. If your work will close up the yard, schedule access first. Drain field replacement, if the field is already failed, is quoted after we inspect the system, not from a landscape sketch. We serve these coordination calls in Lee, Collier, and Charlotte County, plus LaBelle.</p>"
 "<p><a class='btn primary' href='tel:"+TEL+"'>Call "+PHONE+"</a> <a class='btn ghost' href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>Book routine service on ServiceM8<span class='sr'> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></p>",
 LP_FAQ, "Landscapers and pool builders")

LV_FAQ=[
 ("Does FHA or VA always require a septic pump-out?","Not automatically. The lender, the appraiser, and the underwriter decide what a particular loan needs. Some files ask for an inspection or a pump-out; others do not. Call (239) 451-3261 if a party to the loan has asked for a visit."),
 ("Can Express certify that a home meets FHA or VA rules?","No. We pump the tank and, on an inspection with pump-out ($549.99), tell you what we saw. Loan eligibility is not our decision."),
 ("What official rules should I actually read?","For FHA, start with HUD Handbook 4000.1 and Mortgagee Letter 2025-18 on onsite sewage disposal systems. For VA, start with the VA Lenders Handbook, VA Pamphlet 26-7, Chapter 12, Minimum Property Requirements. Links are in the article above."),
 ("The closing is soon and the lid is buried. What now?","Tell us the deadline when you call. Locating and digging a buried lid is $100, plus $50 for each additional hour. Buyers and sellers can also use <a href='/buying-or-selling-a-home-with-septic/'>buying or selling a home with a septic tank</a>."),
]
guide("/fha-va-lender-septic-requirements/","FHA, VA, and Lender Septic Requirements in Southwest Florida | Express Septic",
 "What FHA and VA official guidance says about onsite sewage systems, and how Express can help when a lender asks for a septic inspection with pump-out ($549.99). We do not decide loan eligibility. Call (239) 451-3261.",
 "FHA, VA, and lender septic requirements",
 "<p><strong>If you are buying or refinancing a Southwest Florida home on a septic tank with an FHA loan, a VA loan, or another mortgage, the lender and the appraiser decide what the file needs, not the pumping company.</strong> This page summarizes official starting points and explains the visit we can provide when a party asks for one. It is not legal advice, it is not an underwriting opinion, and it does not promise that any particular report will clear a loan condition. A septic inspection with pump-out is $549.99. Call <a href='tel:"+TEL+"'>"+PHONE+"</a> or <a href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>request a routine visit on ServiceM8</a>.</p>"
 "<h2>What does official FHA guidance say about onsite sewage systems?</h2>"
 "<p>HUD's Single Family Housing Policy Handbook 4000.1 addresses onsite sewage disposal systems. FHA Mortgagee Letter 2025-18, dated June 27, 2025, restates the appraiser's required analysis and reporting on that subject and is published by HUD at <a href='https://www.hud.gov/sites/dfiles/OCHCO/documents/2025-18hsgml.pdf' rel='noopener'>hud.gov</a>. The handbook itself is available from HUD's <a href='https://www.hud.gov/hud-partners/single-family-handbook-4000-1' rel='noopener'>Handbook 4000.1 page</a>.</p>"
 "<p>In that Mortgagee Letter, HUD describes an onsite sewage disposal system as a wastewater system designed to treat and dispose of effluent on the same property that produces the wastewater. The appraiser must notify the mortgagee of an MPR or MPS deficiency if the property is not on an off-site sewer system and a living unit is not provided with an onsite system adequate to dispose of all domestic wastes in a manner that will not create a nuisance or endanger the public health. The appraiser must visually observe the system and its surrounding area, must require an inspection to ensure the system is in proper working order if there are readily observable signs of system failure, and must report on the availability of public sewer to the site. The appraiser must also notify the mortgagee if there is evidence that the system is not sufficient.</p>"
 "<p>That is the appraiser's official starting point, not a rule that every FHA loan automatically includes a pump-out from us. The mortgagee and the appraiser decide what happens next on a given property.</p>"
 "<h2>What does official VA guidance say?</h2>"
 "<p>The Department of Veterans Affairs sets Minimum Property Requirements in the VA Lenders Handbook, VA Pamphlet 26-7, Chapter 12. VA's handbook landing page is <a href='https://www.benefits.va.gov/WARMS/pam26_7.asp' rel='noopener'>benefits.va.gov/WARMS/pam26_7.asp</a>. Chapter 12 includes requirements for a safe method of sewage disposal and for individual sewage disposal systems. VA has also noted that Chapter 12 was updated effective May 1, 2026, so anyone underwriting or appraising a current file should read the current handbook text rather than an old summary, including this one.</p>"
 "<p>We will not invent a checklist of tank sizes, setback distances, or mandatory pump-out intervals and call them current VA rules. The VA-assigned appraiser and the lender decide whether a property meets the MPRs and whether a further inspection or a repair is required. If they ask the parties to have the tank opened or pumped, we can do that work. We cannot certify that the property 'meets VA' in a way that binds the Department.</p>"
 "<h2>What can Express actually provide for a loan file?</h2>"
 "<p>We can perform a septic inspection with pump-out for $549.99, or a planned residential pump-out starting at $399.99 for a standard 1–3 bedroom home if the parties only need the tank emptied and already know the system. We tell you what we saw. We do not decide whether that visit satisfies a loan condition. Home inspectors who are coordinating the same file can use <a href='/septic-for-home-inspectors/'>the inspector partner page</a>. Buyers and sellers can start with <a href='/buying-or-selling-a-home-with-septic/'>buying or selling a home with a septic tank</a>. If the lid is buried, locating and digging is $100, plus $50 for each additional hour.</p>"
 "<h2>What should you do if a condition appears on the appraisal?</h2>"
 "<p>Read the condition, ask the lender what documentation they want, and then call us if they want the tank pumped or inspected. Bring the deadline. After-hours pumping is $599.99 only if you are dealing with an actual backup, not a paperwork deadline. Drain field repairs, if someone is requiring work beyond a pump-out, are quoted after we inspect the system. Conventional lenders and other programs may use still different overlays; those are also the lender's rules, not ours.</p>"
 "<h2>What this page will not tell you?</h2>"
 "<p>We will not guess whether your particular street must hook up to public sewer, whether a 1970s tank is automatically ineligible, or whether a letter on our letterhead will clear underwriting. Official HUD and VA sources, plus your lender, are the authorities. We pump tanks in Lee, Collier, and Charlotte County, plus LaBelle, and we will be plain about what we observed once the lid is open.</p>"
 "<p><a class='btn primary' href='tel:"+TEL+"'>Call "+PHONE+"</a> <a class='btn ghost' href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>Book routine service on ServiceM8<span class='sr'> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></p>",
 LV_FAQ, "FHA, VA, and lender requirements")

PR_FAQ=[
 ("Will a pump-out fix a failed drain field?","It can help if the tank is full or a filter is clogged and that was part of the problem. It will not rebuild a field that has already failed. We will tell you what we saw. Drain field replacement is quoted only after we inspect the system. Call (239) 451-3261."),
 ("How much does drain field replacement cost?","We do not publish a replacement price. That work is quoted after we inspect the system. We also do not invent a typical Southwest Florida total or a 'price per foot' on this page."),
 ("How much does a pump-out cost?","A planned residential pump-out starts at $399.99 (standard 1–3 bed). After-hours pumping is $599.99. A septic inspection with pump-out is $549.99."),
 ("If the yard is wet, is replacement already decided?","No. A wet yard can have more than one cause. See <a href='/drain-field-not-draining/'>drain field not draining</a> and <a href='/wet-spots-green-grass-over-drain-field/'>wet spots and green grass</a>, and have the tank opened before anyone assumes the field is finished."),
]
guide("/pumping-vs-drain-field-replacement/","Septic Pumping vs Drain Field Replacement in Southwest Florida | Express Septic",
 "When a septic pump-out may help, and when a drain field itself may be the problem. Pumping starts at $399.99. Drain field replacement is quoted after inspection. Call (239) 451-3261.",
 "Septic pumping versus drain field replacement",
 "<p><strong>A full tank and a failed drain field can look similar from the bathroom door, but they are not the same repair, and they do not have the same price.</strong> This page is about how to think about that difference without inventing a replacement cost. A planned residential pump-out starts at $399.99 for a standard 1–3 bedroom home. Drain field replacement is quoted only after we inspect the system. Call <a href='tel:"+TEL+"'>"+PHONE+"</a> rather than guessing from a wet patch in the yard.</p>"
 "<h2>What does pumping the tank actually do?</h2>"
 "<p>Pumping removes sludge and scum that have collected in the tank. Express pumps both the inlet and the outlet compartments and cleans an effluent filter at no charge if the tank has one. When the tank is full, or when a clogged filter is holding wastewater back toward the house, emptying it can relieve backups, odors, and extra load on the field. That is why a pump-out is the right first mechanical step in many calls, including some that later turn out to involve the field as well. An inspection with pump-out is $549.99 if you want that visit to include a clearer conversation about what we saw, which is common when a sale is pending or records are missing.</p>"
 "<h2>When is the drain field itself the problem?</h2>"
 "<p>When effluent is already at the surface, when the same footprint stays wet after the rest of the lot dries, when the house still backs up after the tank has just been pumped, or when we can see that the field is not taking water. A high water table in the rainy season can make a marginal field look worse, which is not the same as proof that the field is finished, and it is not a reason to skip looking at the tank. The problem-focused pages are <a href='/drain-field-not-draining/'>drain field not draining</a> and <a href='/wet-spots-green-grass-over-drain-field/'>wet spots and green grass</a>. We will not declare a field failed from a phone photo.</p>"
 "<h2>Why don't we publish a replacement price?</h2>"
 "<p>Drain field work depends on what is in the ground, how the lot is used, and what we find when we inspect. Publishing a made-up 'typical' number, a range we invented, or a cost comparison with pumping would not be honest. The only pricing we will state here for that work is that it is quoted after we inspect the system. The service page for the work itself is <a href='/drain-field-replacement/'>drain field replacement</a>. Pumping prices, risers, and lid locating are published and are listed together on the <a href='/southwest-florida-septic-cost-guide/'>cost guide</a>.</p>"
 "<h2>Which visit should I start with?</h2>"
 "<p>Start with the tank in almost every case: a planned pump-out at the residential starting price above, or an inspection with pump-out if you need a closer look, or an after-hours call at $599.99 if sewage is overflowing now. Do not use the online form during an overflow; call. If lids are buried, locating and digging is $100, plus $50 for each additional hour. After the tank is open, we can talk about whether the field needs more than a pump-out. That order avoids paying for a theory about the field when the tank was simply due.</p>"
 "<h2>What will not settle the question?</h2>"
 "<p>Additives, a new layer of fill on a wet spot, or waiting until the dry season and hoping will not tell you whether the field still works. Additives also do not replace pumping; see <a href='/septic-myths/'>septic myth-busters</a> and <a href='/septic-bacteria-drain-field/'>good bacteria and the drain field</a>. If you are comparing options before a sale, the lender still decides any loan condition; see <a href='/fha-va-lender-septic-requirements/'>FHA, VA, and lender septic requirements</a>. You can <a href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>request a routine visit on ServiceM8</a> when it is not an emergency. We serve homes in Lee, Collier, and Charlotte County, plus LaBelle.</p>"
 "<p><a class='btn primary' href='tel:"+TEL+"'>Call "+PHONE+"</a> <a class='btn ghost' href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>Book routine service on ServiceM8<span class='sr'> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></p>",
 PR_FAQ, "Pumping vs drain field replacement")

CG_FAQ=[
 ("What is the starting price for residential septic pumping?","A planned residential pump-out starts at $399.99 for a standard 1–3 bedroom home. Call (239) 451-3261 or request a time on ServiceM8."),
 ("What does after-hours pumping cost?","After-hours pumping is $599.99. Call (239) 451-3261. Do not use the online booking form during an active backup."),
 ("How much is a septic inspection with pump-out?","$549.99. That visit includes pumping the tank and a look at the system so you hear what we saw."),
 ("Do you publish a price for drain field replacement or lift station pumping?","No. Those are quoted after we inspect the system. We also do not publish prices we have not been given, such as a typical replacement total."),
]
guide("/southwest-florida-septic-cost-guide/","Southwest Florida Septic Service Cost Guide | Express Septic",
 "Published septic service prices for Express in Southwest Florida: pumping from $399.99, after-hours $599.99, inspection with pump-out $549.99, risers, lid locating, and $695 for a 1,000-gallon grease unit. Call (239) 451-3261.",
 "Southwest Florida septic service cost guide",
 "<p><strong>This page collects the septic and grease prices Express actually publishes, and it is honest about the work we only quote after we look at the system.</strong> It is not a market survey of other companies, and it does not invent an 'average Southwest Florida septic bill.' If a number is not on this list, we do not have a published price for it. For a planned residential visit, you can call <a href='tel:"+TEL+"'>"+PHONE+"</a> or <a href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>request a time on ServiceM8</a>.</p>"
 "<h2>What does a planned residential pump-out cost?</h2>"
 "<p>A planned residential pump-out starts at $399.99 for a standard 1–3 bedroom home. That is the published starting point for emptying a household tank on a routine visit. It is not automatically the price for a large commercial tank, a shared park system, a hotel, or a venue. Those visits are quoted for the tank and its use. We pump both the inlet and the outlet compartments, and we clean the effluent filter at no charge if the tank has one. If you only remember one household number, this is the one for a scheduled, non-emergency pump-out that fits that standard starting point.</p>"
 "<h2>What does after-hours pumping cost?</h2>"
 "<p>After-hours pumping is $599.99. Use this when sewage is overflowing, or when you need that after-hours visit, and call rather than booking online. The steps to take in the house are on <a href='/emergency-septic-backup/'>emergency septic backup</a>. After-hours pricing is not a different kind of pumping; it is the published price for that timing.</p>"
 "<h2>What does a septic inspection with pump-out cost?</h2>"
 "<p>A septic inspection with pump-out is $549.99. That is the visit buyers, sellers, and some lenders ask for when they want the tank emptied and a report of what we saw. It is still not a general home inspection or an FHA or VA certification; see <a href='/fha-va-lender-septic-requirements/'>FHA, VA, and lender septic requirements</a> and <a href='/septic-inspections/'>septic inspections</a>. Choose this when records are thin or a contract deadline is in play, not only when the tank is due.</p>"
 "<h2>What do risers and buried-lid locating cost?</h2>"
 "<p>A 6-inch riser is $299.99 each. A riser brings the lid up to ground level so later visits do not start with a shovel. Locating and digging a buried lid is $100, plus $50 for each additional hour. Those access prices are in addition to the pump-out or inspection when both are needed. The longer explanation of buried lids is at <a href='/cant-find-septic-tank-lid/'>can't find the septic tank lid</a>, and riser work is described on <a href='/septic-tank-riser-installation/'>septic tank riser installation</a>.</p>"
 "<h2>What does grease trap or interceptor pumping cost?</h2>"
 "<p>$695 pumps a 1,000-gallon grease trap or interceptor. Other sizes are quoted. We can set a recurring schedule. We do not extrapolate a per-gallon rate from that number. Restaurant and food-service details, including Lee County timing rules, are on <a href='/grease-trap-pumping/'>grease trap and interceptor pumping</a>. Food trucks and commissaries can start at <a href='/septic-for-food-trucks-and-commissary-kitchens/'>food trucks and commissary kitchens</a>.</p>"
 "<h2>What work is only quoted after an inspection?</h2>"
 "<p>Lift station pumping is quoted after we inspect the system; see <a href='/lift-station-pumping/'>lift station pumping</a>. Drain field replacement and septic repairs are also quoted after we inspect; see <a href='/drain-field-replacement/'>drain field replacement</a> and the comparison page on <a href='/pumping-vs-drain-field-replacement/'>pumping versus drain field replacement</a>. We will not invent a typical replacement total, a per-foot field price, or a package discount on this guide. If you need a number for that work, it comes after we look at the property. We serve Lee, Collier, and Charlotte County, plus LaBelle, from 23357 Olde Meadowbrook Circle, Estero, FL 34134.</p>"
 "<p><a class='btn primary' href='tel:"+TEL+"'>Call "+PHONE+"</a> <a class='btn ghost' href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>Book routine service on ServiceM8<span class='sr'> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></p>",
 CG_FAQ, "Septic service cost guide")

TS_FAQ=[
 ("Can you tell me over the phone that my tank is too small?","No. Household size, water use, and what we see in the tank matter, and we will not size a system from a description. Call (239) 451-3261 to schedule a look. An inspection with pump-out is $549.99."),
 ("If we just pumped last year and it is already backing up, is the tank too small?","It can be a sign the tank is undersized for how the house is used, or that something else is wrong, such as a clogged filter, a leak that is filling the tank with extra water, or a field that is not taking effluent. The tank has to be opened before anyone jumps to a single cause."),
 ("Does a large family automatically need a new tank?","Not automatically. More people often means more frequent pumping. Whether the tank or field itself should be changed is a question we can talk about only after we inspect the system."),
 ("What does pumping cost while we figure this out?","A planned residential pump-out starts at $399.99 (standard 1–3 bed). After-hours pumping is $599.99 if you are already overflowing."),
]
guide("/signs-septic-tank-too-small/","Signs Your Septic Tank May Be Too Small | Express Septic",
 "Signs a septic tank may be undersized for how a Southwest Florida home is used, and why we will not size a system over the phone. Pumping starts at $399.99. Call (239) 451-3261.",
 "Signs your septic tank may be too small",
 "<p><strong>A septic tank that is too small for the way a house is actually used will keep filling faster than the calendar says it should, but several other problems can look the same from inside the bathroom.</strong> This page is about the signs that raise that question, and about the limits of what we can say before the tank is open. We will not invent a gallon chart or size a new tank from a phone call. A planned residential pump-out starts at $399.99 for a standard 1–3 bedroom home. Call <a href='tel:"+TEL+"'>"+PHONE+"</a> if the house is already cycling through trouble more often than it should.</p>"
 "<h2>What patterns suggest the tank may be undersized?</h2>"
 "<p>The house backs up or the drains slow again not long after a thorough pump-out, especially when occupancy is high. Holiday weeks and a full multi-generational household bring odors or gurgling even when the last service date looks recent on paper. Laundry and showers cannot be staggered enough to keep the system calm. Those patterns can mean the tank does not have enough room for the solids and water the house produces. They can also mean a clogged filter, a leak adding extra water, or a drain field that is not taking effluent. The pattern is a reason to look, not a diagnosis by itself.</p>"
 "<h2>How is this different from a tank that is simply due to be pumped?</h2>"
 "<p>A tank that has not been pumped in a long time can act 'too small' when it is really just full. The <a href='https://www.epa.gov/septic' rel='noopener'>U.S. EPA SepticSmart</a> guidance of every 3 to 5 years for most household tanks is still the starting point. If you are well inside that window, or you just had the tank emptied, and the house is already in trouble again, size and use deserve a closer look. If you have no idea when it was last pumped, start there: pump first, then watch how the house behaves. An inspection with pump-out is $549.99 when records are missing and you want that conversation documented as well as an empty tank.</p>"
 "<h2>What else can mimic a small tank?</h2>"
 "<p>A dirty effluent filter can make the whole house slow down; we clean the filter at no charge when we pump, and <a href='/septic-filter-care/'>filter care</a> explains why. A garbage disposal or a water softener can add solids or extra water; see <a href='/garbage-disposal-and-water-softener-septic/'>disposals and softeners</a>. A leaking toilet can quietly fill the system. A field that is saturated in the rainy season can send water back toward the house even when the tank was recently pumped; see <a href='/rainy-season-septic/'>rainy season septic care</a> and <a href='/drain-field-not-draining/'>drain field not draining</a>. We sort those possibilities by opening the tank, not by guessing from the bedroom count.</p>"
 "<h2>Do large or multi-generational families always need a bigger system?</h2>"
 "<p>Not always. Many busy houses do fine if they pump on a tighter schedule and spread laundry and showers. The habits for that kind of household are on <a href='/septic-for-large-families/'>large and multi-generational families</a>. A bigger family is a reason to take the schedule seriously, not an automatic order for a new tank. If the system itself is a poor match for how the property is used now, that is something we can discuss only after we inspect. Drain field or tank work beyond pumping is quoted after that inspection. We do not publish a redesign price.</p>"
 "<h2>What should you do if you suspect the tank is undersized?</h2>"
 "<p>Reduce peak water use, stop flushing things that do not belong in the tank, and schedule a visit. If sewage is overflowing, call; after-hours pumping is $599.99. If lids are buried, locating and digging is $100, plus $50 for each additional hour. You can <a href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>request a routine visit on ServiceM8</a> when it is not an emergency. Bring what you know about household size, recent pumping dates, and any recent remodel that added bedrooms or a rental unit. We serve these questions in Lee, Collier, and Charlotte County, plus LaBelle, and we will be conservative about what the evidence actually shows once the lid is open.</p>"
 "<p><a class='btn primary' href='tel:"+TEL+"'>Call "+PHONE+"</a> <a class='btn ghost' href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>Book routine service on ServiceM8<span class='sr'> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></p>",
 TS_FAQ, "Signs your tank may be too small")

MY_FAQ=[
 ("Do septic tank additives replace pumping?","No. The U.S. EPA does not recommend septic system additives containing bacteria or chemicals for ordinary household systems, and additives are not a substitute for pumping. See EPA's Septic Tank Additives Fact Sheet (Publication 830-F-24-003) and <a href='https://www.epa.gov/septic' rel='noopener'>epa.gov/septic</a>. Call (239) 451-3261 to schedule a pump-out."),
 ("Is it okay to never pump a septic tank?","No. Solids collect in the tank over time. Leaving it unpumped can lead to backups and solids moving toward the drain field. The EPA's typical interval for household tanks is every 3 to 5 years, depending on use and tank size."),
 ("Can I use the toilet or run water while you are pumping?","Wait until the crew tells you it is all right. Extra water during the pump-out can interfere with the work. If you are unsure, ask the technician on site before you flush."),
 ("Do I have to pump after every hurricane?","No. Pump if the tank was already due, or have the system checked after floodwater recedes and the lids are reachable. A special trip is not a rule we invent for every storm watch. See <a href='/hurricane-and-flood-septic-prep/'>hurricane and flood septic prep</a>."),
 ("Is septic-safe toilet paper really safe to flush?","Septic-safe, biodegradable toilet paper is a better choice than thick wipes or paper towels. It is not a license to flush wipes, even ones labeled flushable, or to skip pumping."),
]
guide("/septic-myths/","Septic System Myths in Southwest Florida | Express Septic",
 "Straight answers to common septic myths: additives and EPA guidance, never pumping, using the toilet during a pump-out, pumping after a hurricane, and septic-safe toilet paper. Call (239) 451-3261.",
 "Septic system myths, answered",
 "<p><strong>Southwest Florida homeowners hear a lot of septic advice that is only half true, and some of it can delay a pump-out that the tank actually needs.</strong> This page answers five of those questions in one place: additives, never pumping, using the toilet during a visit, pumping after a hurricane, and 'septic-safe' toilet paper. It is not a science journal, and it is not a substitute for opening the tank when the system is in trouble. A planned residential pump-out starts at $399.99 for a standard 1–3 bedroom home. Call <a href='tel:"+TEL+"'>"+PHONE+"</a> or <a href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>request a routine visit on ServiceM8</a> when you want the work done rather than another opinion.</p>"
 "<h2>Do septic tank additives work, and can they replace pumping?</h2>"
 "<p>The U.S. Environmental Protection Agency does not recommend septic system additives containing bacteria or chemicals for ordinary domestic wastewater treatment. EPA's Septic Tank Additives Fact Sheet, Publication 830-F-24-003 (updated 2024), says there is already a significant presence of bacteria, enzymes, yeasts, fungi, and other microorganisms in a septic system, and that additive products can be ineffective or even harm system operation and the environment. EPA also notes that some products may interfere with the breakdown of waste, contribute to clogging, and contaminate groundwater and receiving waters, and that many claims have only limited third-party research. The fact sheet is published at <a href='https://www.epa.gov/system/files/documents/2024-09/septictankadditivesfactsheet.pdf' rel='noopener'>epa.gov</a>; EPA's septic overview is at <a href='https://www.epa.gov/septic' rel='noopener'>epa.gov/septic</a>.</p>"
 "<p>That is why we do not sell a bottle as a substitute for a pump-out. A healthy system normally gets the bacteria it needs from household waste. If the tank is full, the tank needs to be pumped. For a fuller explanation of anaerobic and aerobic bacteria and the biomat, use <a href='/septic-bacteria-drain-field/'>good bacteria and the drain field</a>, which is the educational page for that topic. This section stays on the myth that a product can take the place of service.</p>"
 "<h2>Is it okay to never pump a septic tank?</h2>"
 "<p>No. Sludge and scum accumulate in the tank over time. If they are not removed, they can take up the room the tank needs to settle solids, and solids can move toward the outlet and the drain field. EPA's SepticSmart guidance is that most household tanks are pumped every 3 to 5 years, with the interval depending on household size, water use, and tank size. 'Never' is not an interval. A tank that has not been opened in a decade is not proof that the system is exceptional; it is a reason to schedule service before the house teaches you the hard way. See <a href='/how-often-to-pump-septic-tank/'>how often to pump</a> for the existing frequency discussion, including the chart already on that page. We do not invent a new chart here.</p>"
 "<h2>Can I use the toilet during a pump-out?</h2>"
 "<p>Wait until the crew says it is all right. While the tank is being opened and pumped, extra water from a toilet, a shower, or a washing machine can interfere with the work and can surprise the people at the lid. If you need to know when you can flush, ask the technician on site rather than guessing from a video or a neighbor's story. After the visit is finished and the lids are closed, normal use can resume unless the crew has told you otherwise, for example if the drain field is already saturated and they have asked you to go easy on water. This is courtesy and safety on a job, not a superstition.</p>"
 "<h2>Do I need to pump after every hurricane?</h2>"
 "<p>Not automatically. If a pump-out was already due, or the tank was close to full before the storm, pumping when we can reach you can help. If the tank was recently emptied, a special trip is not a rule we invent for every watch or warning. After a flood, do not use the system while standing water covers the tank or drain field; wait until the water recedes and the lids are reachable, then have the system checked before you return to normal use. That is the conservative after-storm approach, and the step-by-step version lives on <a href='/hurricane-and-flood-septic-prep/'>hurricane and flood septic prep</a>. After-hours pumping is $599.99 if you have a backup; call rather than booking online during an overflow.</p>"
 "<h2>Is septic-safe toilet paper really safe?</h2>"
 "<p>Septic-safe, biodegradable toilet paper is a better everyday choice than thick, 'quilted' paper that breaks down slowly, and it is far better than flushing wipes or paper towels. It is not a magic product that lets you ignore the tank, and it does not make wipes labeled 'flushable' acceptable. Those wipes are still a no. Feminine products, paper towels, and dental floss still belong in the trash. If your only change is a different toilet-paper brand, and you never pump, the tank will still fill. The broader list of what never to flush is in the <a href='/first-time-septic-owner/'>first-time septic guide</a>. Pumping, a clean filter, and decent habits do more than any roll of paper can do on its own.</p>"
 "<h2>What should you do instead of relying on a myth?</h2>"
 "<p>Know your last pump-out date, keep lids findable (a riser is $299.99 each if they keep disappearing), and call when drains slow, the yard smells, or an alarm sounds. An inspection with pump-out is $549.99 when you want a current look, not only an empty tank. If you cannot find the lid, locating and digging is $100, plus $50 for each additional hour. We serve homes in Lee, Collier, and Charlotte County, plus LaBelle. If a backup is happening now, stop running water and call <a href='tel:"+TEL+"'>"+PHONE+"</a>.</p>"
 "<p><a class='btn primary' href='tel:"+TEL+"'>Call "+PHONE+"</a> <a class='btn ghost' href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>Book routine service on ServiceM8<span class='sr'> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></p>",
 MY_FAQ, "Septic myths")


# --- Phase 3 group 1: restaurant grease trap & interceptor city pages ---
COLLIER_FOG='<a href="https://www.collier.gov/Business-Resources/Public-Utilities/Wastewater-Pretreatment/Food-Service-Establishments" rel="noopener">Collier County Food Service Establishments Program</a>'
BSU_POL='<a href="https://bsu.us/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/Final-Sewer-Policy-2019.pdf" rel="noopener">Bonita Springs Utilities Sewer Use Policy</a> (BOD approved August 2019)'
BSU_CARD='<a href="https://bsu.us/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/GREASE-INTERCEPTOR-MAINTENANCE-CARD-FORM-rev-2022-04-12.pdf" rel="noopener">BSU grease interceptor maintenance card</a>'
BSU_AREA='<a href="https://bsu.us/our-service-area/" rel="noopener">BSU franchise service area</a>'
