grease_city("/grease-trap-pumping-cape-coral-fl/","Grease Trap Pumping in Cape Coral, FL | Interceptor Cleaning | Express Septic",
 "Grease trap and interceptor pumping in Cape Coral, FL. $695 for a 1,000-gallon unit. Cape Coral Utilities sets the FOG schedule for city sewer customers; confirm current requirements. Call (239) 451-3261.",
 "Grease trap and interceptor pumping in Cape Coral, FL",
 "<p><strong>Cape Coral restaurant corridors follow City of Cape Coral Utilities, not a paste of Lee County Ordinance 14-24, unless the kitchen is actually billed by Lee County Utilities.</strong> This page is for food-service establishments in Cape Coral, including along Cape Coral Parkway, Del Prado Boulevard, and Pine Island Road. Cape Coral's 1,000-gallon pumping price is $695; other sizes are quoted, not prorated from that number. Call <a href='tel:"+TEL+"'>"+PHONE+"</a> or <a href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>request a Cape Coral visit on ServiceM8</a>.</p>"
 "<h2>Where are Cape Coral kitchens concentrated?</h2>"
 "<p>Cape Coral Parkway and Del Prado carry much of the city's sit-down and center-based food service. Pine Island Road and the mid-Cape commercial pockets add more kitchens that serve neighborhoods built around the canal grid. Seasonal residents and weekend boat traffic change how busy those rooms get in winter. We do not list private restaurants as customers. Fort Myers kitchens across the bridges should use <a href='/grease-trap-pumping-fort-myers-fl/'>grease trap pumping in Fort Myers</a>.</p>"
 "<h2>Who sets FOG rules on Cape Coral sewer?</h2>"
 "<p>Cape Coral Utilities administers water and sewer service for typical city customers, and the city's Code of Ordinances includes a Water and Sewer Utilities chapter (Chapter 19) plus related permit language. During this research we did not independently retrieve a primary city code section we could quote for a specific grease-trap or interceptor pumping frequency, such as every 30 days, every 90 days, or a 25% capacity trigger. Numbers like those appear on third-party sites; we will not copy them here. Confirm the current schedule with Cape Coral Utilities.</p>"
 "<h2>When would Ordinance 14-24 apply?</h2>"
 "<p>"+LEE_ORD+" applies to food-service establishments discharging to Lee County Utilities: interceptors fully pumped at least every 90 calendar days, and indoor traps cleaned at least weekly or whenever grease and solids exceed 20% of the unit volume. That is not automatically the Cape Coral city-sewer calendar. Check the bill. Do not apply Bonita Springs Utilities' 25% wetted-height interceptor rule unless BSU actually serves the account, which is not the typical Cape Coral case.</p>"
 "<h2>What Florida law still applies in Cape Coral?</h2>"
 "<p>Cape Coral kitchens are still under "+FL_STATUTE+": the pump-out must have a signed grease waste service manifest, and gray-water return into the unit is prohibited. This city page is a pumping and scheduling page, not a claim that we issue manifests, hold grease-hauler licensing, or run compliance reporting. After you confirm Cape Coral Utilities' current interval, we can offer a repeating route. If a lid has to be found and dug, the charge is $100 plus $50 for each additional hour. An in-progress after-hours backup is $599.99; call the number rather than submitting the form.</p>"
 "<h2>Canals, lots, and related pages</h2>"
 "<p>Cape Coral lots are often tight, and some interceptors sit near a canal-side parking pad or behind a center enclosure. Tell us about access and lids under pavers. Household septic work is on <a href='/septic-tank-pumping-cape-coral-fl/'>septic tank pumping in Cape Coral</a>. A full city list is on the <a href='/grease-trap-pumping-service-areas/'>grease service-areas hub</a>.</p>"
 "<h2>Bridges, canals, and why the city utility matters</h2>"
 "<p>Cape Coral is a planned canal city. Restaurant traffic crosses the Cape Coral, Midpoint, and Veterans Memorial bridges from Fort Myers, especially on winter weekends, which is why a midweek early-morning pump-out on Cape Coral Parkway is often easier than a Saturday dinner rush. The canals also mean some pads are tight and some hose runs are longer than a typical inland plaza. None of that changes the price: $695 still pumps a 1,000-gallon unit, and other sizes are still quoted. What it does change is the need to describe access when you call. Because Cape Coral Utilities is the usual sewer provider inside the city, this page will not paste Lee County Ordinance 14-24 or Bonita Springs Utilities' 25% wetted-height rule onto a Cape account. Confirm the current city interval, then we can match a recurring route to it.</p>"
 "<p class='srcs'>Sources: "+FL_STATUTE+"; "+LEE_ORD+" (only if the kitchen discharges to Lee County Utilities). Confirm Cape Coral Utilities' current FOG schedule with the city.</p>"
 "<p><a class='btn primary' href='tel:"+TEL+"'>Call "+PHONE+"</a> <a class='btn ghost' href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>Book a Cape Coral visit on ServiceM8<span class='sr'> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></p>",
 CCG_FAQ, "Cape Coral grease trap pumping")

