 "<p><strong>Lehigh Acres is unincorporated Lee County, so restaurant kitchens on Lee County Utilities follow Ordinance 14-24 rather than a city FOG ordinance of their own.</strong> This page is for food-service establishments in Lehigh Acres, including along Lee Boulevard, Homestead Road, and other inland commercial streets. In Lehigh Acres, a 1,000-gallon grease trap or interceptor is $695 to pump, and other sizes get a quote. Call <a href='tel:"+TEL+"'>"+PHONE+"</a> or <a href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>request a Lehigh Acres visit on ServiceM8</a>.</p>"
 "<h2>Where are Lehigh Acres kitchens?</h2>"
 "<p>Most food service sits along Lee Boulevard and at shopping and plaza sites that serve a large, still-growing residential grid. Lehigh is inland, not a beach town, so the visitor spike is milder than on Fort Myers Beach or Marco Island, but weekends, school calendars, and winter residents still change how hard a trap works. We do not invent restaurant names as customers, and we do not recycle job photos from household septic work as if they were grease jobs. Fort Myers and Gateway kitchens should use those city pages; the <a href='/grease-trap-pumping-service-areas/'>service-areas hub</a> lists them.</p>"
 "<h2>What does Ordinance 14-24 require here?</h2>"
 "<p>If the kitchen discharges to Lee County Utilities, "+LEE_ORD+" requires grease interceptors to be fully pumped at least every 90 calendar days. Indoor grease traps must be cleaned at least weekly or whenever grease and solids exceed 20% of the unit volume. The 20% rule attaches to indoor traps, not to interceptors. That is the local schedule this page can cite with confidence for typical LCU accounts in unincorporated Lehigh Acres. If a kitchen is on a package plant or another provider, confirm that provider instead of assuming 14-24.</p>"
 "<h2>What Florida law still applies?</h2>"
 "<p>"+FL_STATUTE+" still applies inland: a signed grease waste service manifest must go with the pump-out, and grease waste that has been removed may not be returned to the interceptor or trap. We can line up a repeating Lehigh route with the 90-day interceptor clock, or with a tighter indoor-trap clock when that is what 14-24 requires. We are not advertising manifest issuance, grease-hauler licensing, or compliance reporting on this page.</p>"
 "<h2>How is Lehigh access different from the beach towns?</h2>"
 "<p>Lots are usually more open than downtown Naples or Estero Boulevard, but lids can still be buried in gravel or grass behind a plaza. In Lehigh, locating and digging that lid is $100, and each additional hour is $50. After-hours pumping is $599.99 if a backup is already happening; call (239) 451-3261 rather than using the online form during an overflow. Household septic work, which is very common in Lehigh, is a different service and is covered on <a href='/septic-tank-pumping-lehigh-acres-fl/'>septic tank pumping in Lehigh Acres</a>. Our truck uses a split 4,200-gallon tank so grease and septic loads are not mixed.</p>"
 "<h2>Why an inland grid still needs a restaurant FOG calendar</h2>"
 "<p>Lehigh Acres does not have a beach-town visitor peak, but it does have a large, still-filling residential grid that supports the kitchens along Lee Boulevard, Homestead Road, and the plaza sites in between. School calendars, weekend family traffic, and winter residents still change how hard those traps work. There is no city-owned sewer utility writing a separate Lehigh FOG ordinance, which is why this page cites Ordinance 14-24 for typical Lee County Utilities accounts instead of a municipal program like Cape Coral Utilities or City of Fort Myers Pretreatment. If a kitchen shares a property with a septic tank for restrooms or a house, say so when you book; those are separate tanks and separate jobs, even if they sit in the same lot. Food-service operators who also use a commissary can see <a href='/septic-for-food-trucks-and-commissary-kitchens/'>food trucks and commissary kitchens</a>.</p>"
 "<p class='srcs'>Sources: "+LEE_ORD+"; "+FL_STATUTE+".</p>"
