 "<h2>Access, after-hours, and septic at the same address</h2>"
 "<p>Some plazas along Business 41 have interceptors at the rear, past delivery stalls. Tell us if a lid is under asphalt or landscaping. North of the river, locating and digging a buried lid costs $100, plus $50 for each additional hour. After-hours pumping is $599.99 if a backup is already happening; call instead of booking online during an overflow. Household septic work in this community is on <a href='/septic-tank-pumping-north-fort-myers-fl/'>septic tank pumping in North Fort Myers</a>. More cities are listed on the <a href='/grease-trap-pumping-service-areas/'>grease service-areas hub</a>.</p>"
 "<h2>North of the river is not the same jurisdiction as downtown</h2>"
 "<p>It is easy to treat 'Fort Myers' as one restaurant market because Hancock Bridge Parkway and the other river crossings feed downtown so quickly. For FOG rules, the line matters. North Fort Myers is unincorporated. A kitchen on Business 41 or Pine Island Road that discharges to Lee County Utilities follows Ordinance 14-24, including the 90-calendar-day interceptor interval and the weekly-or-20% indoor-trap rule. A kitchen that has already crossed into the City of Fort Myers, or west into Cape Coral, is usually under a different utility program, and those city pages are written that way on purpose. Winter traffic still builds on the bridges and on Business 41; a mid-morning weekday visit is often easier than a Friday evening. If you are not sure which side of the city limit you are on, read the utility bill or tell us the address when you call and we will help you pick the right local page, not invent a blended rule.</p>"
