filter_city("/septic-filter-cleaning-naples-fl/","Septic Filter Cleaning in Naples, FL | Effluent Filter Care | Express Septic",
 "Effluent filter cleaning for Naples-area homes still on septic, including larger-lot properties outside central sewer. Filter cleaned free at pump-out. Risers $299.99 each. Call (239) 451-3261.",
 "Septic filter cleaning in Naples, FL",
 "<p><strong>Inside the City of Naples, many houses are on municipal sewer and have no septic effluent filter at all.</strong> The homes that still need this work are usually outside central sewer, including larger-lot properties and areas such as those described on our <a href='/septic-tank-pumping-naples-fl/'>Naples septic pumping</a> page. If that is your house, a clogged outlet filter is one of the most common reasons a drain fails without much warning. We clean the filter at no charge during a pump-out and can show you how to keep it clear. Call <a href='tel:"+TEL+"'>"+PHONE+"</a> or <a href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>use ServiceM8 to request a Naples visit</a>.</p>"
 "<h2>How can you tell if a Naples house even has a filter to clean?</h2>"
 "<p>If you pay a sewer charge to the City of Naples, you are not looking for a tank filter. If you do not, and wastewater stays on the property, you likely have a septic tank. Not every tank was built with an effluent filter; older units may lack one, and some later tanks have a cartridge in the outlet baffle. We will tell you what is actually in the outlet when we open it, rather than assuming every Naples-area tank is the same. If there is a filter, it should be cleaned as part of the pump-out, and again if fixtures start to drain slowly. That is the whole interval we will publish. We will not invent a monthly or quarterly number.</p>"
 "<h2>What a dirty filter does in a house that sits quiet, then fills up</h2>"
 "<p>Many Naples-area houses are used harder in winter than in late summer. A filter can go from acceptable to restrictive in the first busy weeks after the house is opened. Slow sinks, a toilet that gurgles, or wastewater in a shower stall are the usual signs. The tank may still have room, but the outlet cannot pass the flow. That is an unexpected backup from the homeowner's point of view, even if the filter has been loading for a long time. If sewage is already backing up after hours, pumping is $599.99; call and skip the online form.</p>"
 "<h2>Finding the outlet lid under Naples landscaping</h2>"
 "<p>On larger lots the tank can sit well off the house, and tropical planting, gravel, or a paver path can cover the lids. The filter is under the outlet lid, which is not always the lid closest to the house. If we have to locate and dig, the charge is $100, plus $50 for each additional hour. Photos from a prior pump-out, a marked sprinkler head, or a riser already in place all help. A <a href='/septic-tank-riser-installation/'>6-inch riser</a> at $299.99 each brings that lid to grade so a later filter check does not mean reopening a bed of planting.</p>"
 "<h2>The drain field still needs what the filter is supposed to catch</h2>"
 "<p>Collier County's wet season raises the water table. A drain field that is already working in wet soil has less margin if solids slip past a neglected filter. We are not assigning a failure statistic to Naples fields. We are saying the filter is there to keep sludge in the tank, and cleaning it is part of protecting the field you already have. More general filter guidance is on <a href='/septic-filter-care/'>septic filter care</a>. If the house is actually in North Naples or Golden Gate Estates, those pages may fit better; see the <a href='/septic-filter-cleaning-service-areas/'>filter service-areas hub</a>.</p>"
 "<p><a class='btn primary' href='tel:"+TEL+"'>Call "+PHONE+"</a> <a class='btn ghost' href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>Book a Naples filter visit on ServiceM8<span class='sr'> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></p>",
 NPF_FAQ, "Naples filter cleaning")

