 "<p>Lee County has noted that many Lehigh Acres homes were built from the 1960s through the early 1980s. Some of those tanks were never fitted with an effluent filter. Some were retrofitted. Some newer houses have a cartridge that is easy to miss if you have never opened the outlet lid. We will tell you what is actually there. If there is a filter, clean it when we pump, and clean it again if drains slow. That is the interval we will publish, not a monthly number. Lehigh is flat, and the summer rainy season raises the water table, so a clogged filter in June or July leaves even less margin. If a backup is already happening after hours, pumping is $599.99; call.</p>"
 "<h2>Finding lids on the Lehigh grid</h2>"
 "<p>Lots along Lee Boulevard, Homestead Road, and the residential grid can hide lids in grass, gravel, or a back corner that has not been opened in years. The filter is under the outlet lid, which may not be the lid nearest the front door. Locating and digging a buried lid is $100, plus $50 for each additional hour. A <a href='/septic-tank-riser-installation/'>6-inch riser</a> at $299.99 each is a practical upgrade on a mowed lot if you want to check the filter the next time a sink slows, without starting from a shovel. If you do not know when the tank was last pumped, start with a pump-out; a septic inspection with pump-out is $549.99.</p>"
 "<h2>What the visit includes</h2>"
 "<p>We pump, we clean the filter when one is present, and we can show you how it seats. We do not treat that as a substitute for drain-field replacement if the field has already failed. More general steps are on <a href='/septic-filter-care/'>septic filter care</a>. Other communities are listed on the <a href='/septic-filter-cleaning-service-areas/'>filter service-areas hub</a>.</p>"
