 "<h2>Does a sewer-conversion street still need both chambers emptied?</h2>"
 "<p>Yes, until the house is tied into Lee County Utilities sewer. Early Village phases include package-plant communities and Broadway Avenue areas, and connection is treated as mandatory once sewer reaches the property. That does not retire the outlet chamber early. Leaving sludge in the outlet while you wait for a connection date is how solids get a path toward a field that still has to work, including in a community that has talked about protecting the Estero River as part of why conversions are happening. We do not invent a nutrient number for a half-pumped tank. We do empty both sides of the tank you still have.</p>"
 "<h2>How the two chambers split the work</h2>"
 "<p>The inlet is where raw wastewater arrives and most solids separate. The outlet is the quieter chamber that should be sending clearer water to the drain field, often through a filter in the outlet baffle. A visit that stops at the inlet leaves the second chamber holding whatever has already passed the baffle. That leftover material is what can clog the filter or travel into the field. On an Estero lot with an HOA yard or a tighter building envelope, that field usually does not have spare room to absorb a load it was never meant to take.</p>"
 "<h2>Access in Village yards is about reaching both lids, not just one</h2>"
