both_city("/why-we-pump-both-tanks-cape-coral-fl/","Why We Pump Both Septic Tank Compartments in Cape Coral, FL | Express Septic",
 "Why Express empties both inlet and outlet chambers on remaining Cape Coral septic tanks, especially in the North Cape and on canal lots. Residential pumping from $399.99. Call (239) 451-3261.",
 "Why we pump both septic tank compartments in Cape Coral, FL",
 "<p><strong>Cape Coral began as a low-density community on septic tanks and shallow wells, and many North Cape houses still have those tanks, so both chambers still need to be emptied until the street is actually on city sewer.</strong> Streets that already have city utilities do not have an inlet or outlet septic chamber. This page is for the lots that still do, including canal-front parcels. We pump both compartments, and we clean the filter when one is present. Call <a href='tel:"+TEL+"'>"+PHONE+"</a> or <a href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>request a Cape Coral pump-out on ServiceM8</a>.</p>"
 "<h2>Which lots still have chambers to empty, and which do not?</h2>"
 "<p>The City's Utilities Extension Project has brought water and sewer to much of the Cape and continues working north. Look at the bill rather than assuming your block still has a tank, or assuming it does not. If you pay a city sewer charge, this page is not for your house. If you do not, you likely still have a tank; we will empty both the inlet and the outlet when the tank is built that way. Connection and abandonment questions go to City Customer Billing Services at (239) 242-3853. We do not invent a completion date for your street.</p>"
 "<h2>Canal lots, shallow wells, and what a skipped outlet can cause</h2>"
 "<p>The City has noted that failed septic effluent can reach groundwater and canals. The inlet chamber is where solids should be held. The outlet chamber is the last place those solids should be allowed to remain. A pump-out that stops at the inlet can leave sludge and scum in the outlet, where they can move toward a drain field that shares the lot with a well or sits beside a canal, or pack a filter and send wastewater back into the house. Cleaning a filter later does not retrieve solids that already left the tank. Emptying both chambers is the step we treat as standard, not optional, while the lot still depends on that system.</p>"
 "<h2>Access on canal-side pads</h2>"
 "<p>Two compartments usually mean two lids, and on a canal pad those covers can sit in a tight side yard, under a dock-side enclosure, or under growth along the seawall. Tell us about access, including a boat lift or a narrow easement, when you book. If a lid has to be located and uncovered, that work is $100, plus $50 for each extra hour. Reaching the second lid is how the outlet gets emptied, not a different service line.</p>"
 "<h2>What to ask while you wait on sewer, and what the visit includes</h2>"
 "<p>Ask whether both chambers will be pumped, and whether the filter will be cleaned if the tank has one. We include that filter cleaning in the pump-out and can demonstrate how it seats. We start residential pumping at $399.99 for a standard 1 to 3 bedroom tank and confirm the amount for yours by phone. After-hours pumping during an active backup is $599.99; call. The city list is on the <a href='/why-we-pump-both-tanks-service-areas/'>why we pump both tanks hub</a>. North Fort Myers, across the river, has its own page. Filter-only Cape notes are on <a href='/septic-filter-cleaning-cape-coral-fl/'>filter cleaning in Cape Coral</a>.</p>"
 "<p><a class='btn primary' href='tel:"+TEL+"'>Call "+PHONE+"</a> <a class='btn ghost' href='"+BOOK+"' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>Book a Cape Coral both-chamber pump-out on ServiceM8<span class='sr'> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></p>",
 CCBOTH_FAQ, "Cape Coral both-compartment pumping")

