 "<h2>Neighborhood lots and rural lots hide the second lid differently</h2>"
 "<p>A complete pump-out of a two-compartment tank needs both lids. In a compact neighborhood yard, one cover may be obvious and the other under sod or a shed edge. On a rural parcel the second lid can be a long hose run past a gate or a tree line. Tell us which kind of lot you have, and mention a locked entrance or soft ground after rain. If we have to locate and uncover a buried lid, that work is $100, plus $50 for each extra hour. Skipping the harder lid is how the outlet chamber gets left full.</p>"
 "<h2>What a dirty outlet chamber does when the yard is already wet</h2>"
 "<p>The inlet is where most solids should stay. The outlet is the last chamber before the drain field. If sludge and scum remain there after a one-sided visit, they can move toward a field that is already working in a high water table near the river, or pack a filter and send wastewater back into the house. A wet yard does not replace a complete pump-out; it makes the skipped chamber more consequential. We empty both sides. We do not invent a statistic for river-side failures after incomplete pump-outs.</p>"
 "<h2>Do not use the Fort Myers city page by habit</h2>"
 "<p>If the house is south of the Caloosahatchee, or already on city utilities, open <a href='/why-we-pump-both-tanks-fort-myers-fl/'>why we pump both compartments in Fort Myers</a> and check the sewer bill there. The Cape is a separate remaining-tank story. North Fort Myers is unincorporated, with neighborhood streets and rural parcels that still rely on a tank. Match the page to the lot, not to a familiar mailing name.</p>"
 "<h2>Commuter corridors change the clock, not the compartments</h2>"
 "<p>Crossing to a lot north of the river can be slower at the end of a weekday. If a mid-morning window is easier for you, say so when you book. That choice does not change the work: we empty the inlet and the outlet, and we clean the filter if the tank has one. That filter cleaning is part of the pump-out, and we can demonstrate how it sits. For a standard 1 to 3 bedroom tank, residential pumping starts at $399.99, which we confirm when you call. If a backup is already happening after hours, pumping is $599.99; use the phone.</p>"
 "<h2>What to ask before you book</h2>"
 "<p>Ask whether both compartments will be opened and emptied, not only the inlet, and whether the filter will be cleaned if one is there. 'Pump the tank' is too vague for a two-chamber system. Other cities are listed on the <a href='/why-we-pump-both-tanks-service-areas/'>why we pump both tanks hub</a>. Filter-only notes for this area are on <a href='/septic-filter-cleaning-north-fort-myers-fl/'>filter cleaning in North Fort Myers</a>. General pumping is on <a href='/septic-tank-pumping-north-fort-myers-fl/'>septic tank pumping in North Fort Myers</a>.</p>"
