 "<h2>Why leftover outlet solids are a bad match for a barrier-island field</h2>"
 "<p>Sanibel is a barrier-island city with beaches, shelling, and a wildlife refuge, and much of the island sits low. Wastewater still arrives at the inlet first, where heavier material drops and lighter material rises. The second chamber is the last stop before that sandy field, and often before an effluent filter. If a visit only works the first lid, sludge and scum can remain there and then move into the field, or pack the filter until fixtures slow. We do not invent a number of island fields damaged this way. We treat both chambers as the job.</p>"
