 "<h2>Seasonal houses and the first week back</h2>"
 "<p>A house that sat quiet can fill quickly once winter occupants or short-term guests arrive. Slow drains in that first week are a reason to look at whether both chambers were emptied on the last visit, not a reason to invent a required month that fits every seasonal Sanibel household. We will not publish a numbered interval on this page. If the tank is two-compartment, both sides still get emptied.</p>"
 "<h2>Captiva, Fort Myers Beach, and Marco are different pages</h2>"
 "<p>Captiva sits across Blind Pass and has its own pumping page. Fort Myers Beach and Marco Island are other coastal communities with their own both-compartment pages. Use the Sanibel page when the house is on this island and still on a tank. Mention sand, plantings, or a hard-to-see cover when you book; a lid that has to be located and dug is priced from $100, plus $50 per additional hour. See the full city list on the <a href='/why-we-pump-both-tanks-service-areas/'>why we pump both tanks hub</a>. Where a tank still applies, household pumping is on <a href='/septic-tank-pumping-sanibel-fl/'>septic tank pumping on Sanibel</a>. Outlet-filter notes are on <a href='/septic-filter-cleaning-sanibel-fl/'>filter cleaning on Sanibel</a>.</p>"
